{
  "title": "✖️ Cross-Multiplication — The Formula Method",
  "hook": "What if there was a single formula that could solve any pair of linear equations instantly? The cross-multiplication method gives you exactly that — plug in the coefficients and out come x and y!",
  "explanation": "<p>For the system <b>a₁x + b₁y + c₁ = 0</b> and <b>a₂x + b₂y + c₂ = 0</b>:</p><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Cross-Multiplication Formula</div><strong>x/(b₁c₂ − b₂c₁) = y/(c₁a₂ − c₂a₁) = 1/(a₁b₂ − a₂b₁)</strong></div><p><b>Memory Trick — the arrow diagram:</b></p><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\"></th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">x</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">y</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">1</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Numerator</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">b₁c₂ − b₂c₁</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">c₁a₂ − c₂a₁</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">a₁b₂ − a₂b₁</td></tr></table><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Write coefficients in order: b, c, a, b. Cross-multiply downward-right minus downward-left for each column.</div><div style=\"background:#FFF3E0;border-left:4px solid #E65100;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">⚠️ <strong>Tip:</strong> Equations MUST be in the form ax + by + c = 0 (everything on one side). Move constants to the left!</div><div style=\"background:#F5F5F5;border-left:3px solid #4A90D9;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;margin:6px 0;font-size:0.9rem\">This works when a₁b₂ − a₂b₁ ≠ 0 (the lines are not parallel).</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> Solve 2x + 3y − 8 = 0 and 4x + 5y − 14 = 0.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>List coefficients</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">a₁=2, b₁=3, c₁=−8; a₂=4, b₂=5, c₂=−14</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Compute numerators</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">For x: b₁c₂ − b₂c₁ = 3(−14) − 5(−8) = −42 + 40 = −2</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong></strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">For y: c₁a₂ − c₂a₁ = (−8)(4) − (−14)(2) = −32 + 28 = −4</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Compute denominator</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">a₁b₂ − a₂b₁ = 2(5) − 4(3) = 10 − 12 = −2</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div><div><strong>Calculate</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">x = −2/(−2) = 1, y = −4/(−2) = 2</span></div></div></div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><strong>Solution: x = 1, y = 2</strong></div><p>Verify: 2(1) + 3(2) = 8 ✓, 4(1) + 5(2) = 14 ✓</p>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Solve using cross-multiplication: x + 2y − 1 = 0 and 2x − 3y − 12 = 0.",
    "hint": "a₁=1, b₁=2, c₁=−1; a₂=2, b₂=−3, c₂=−12. Apply the formula.",
    "answer": "x-numerator: 2(−12) − (−3)(−1) = −24 − 3 = −27. y-numerator: (−1)(2) − (−12)(1) = −2 + 12 = 10. Denominator: 1(−3) − 2(2) = −3 − 4 = −7. x = −27/(−7) = 27/7, y = 10/(−7) = −10/7."
  },
  "fun_fact": "Cross-multiplication is actually Cramer's Rule in disguise — named after Gabriel Cramer (1704-1752), though the method was known in China centuries earlier!"
}